# Canada — Geography
# answer-mode: type
# answer-case: insensitive
# Cloze cards ({{...}}, no separator) can't carry notes — the note syntax
# needs the --- separators. Facts that deserve a note are typed cards here.

Canada's longest river is the {{Mackenzie}}.

What is Canada's highest peak?
---
Mount Logan
= Logan
---
5,959 m, in Yukon's Kluane National Park. Second-highest in North America
after Denali.

The {{Canadian Shield}} covers roughly half of Canada's land area.
= Shield

Which Great Lake lies entirely outside Canada?
---
Michigan
= Lake Michigan
---
The other four all straddle the border. Ontario touches every Great Lake
except Michigan.

The {{St. Lawrence}} River connects the Great Lakes to the Atlantic.
= Saint Lawrence

Which bay has the highest tides in the world?
---
Bay of Fundy
= Fundy
---
Between New Brunswick and Nova Scotia — up to 16 metres between low and
high tide.

How many provinces does Canada have?
---
10
= ten
---
Plus three territories: Yukon, the Northwest Territories, and Nunavut.

How many US states share a border with Canada?
---
13
= thirteen
---
From Alaska to Maine. Ontario alone borders five of them.

Much of the Canada–US border follows the {{49th}} parallel.

The three prairie provinces are Alberta, Saskatchewan, and {{Manitoba}}.

The only officially bilingual province is {{New Brunswick}}.

Which territory was created in 1999?
---
Nunavut
---
Carved from the eastern Northwest Territories — the largest internal
boundary change since Newfoundland joined in 1949.

Canada has the world's longest {{coastline}}.

Which province joined Confederation last, in 1949?
---
Newfoundland and Labrador
= Newfoundland
